00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:28.610
I think you can apply these concepts and ideas to many other things. The purpose of a story, I think, is to convey information in a way that lets the reader contextualize it in their own life. It's not just to have a data dump of numbers and facts. It's to allow someone to read numbers and facts and information and say, I know what that would feel like. I can feel that in my own life. You're not just conveying data, you're conveying emotion to somebody.

34
00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:57.710
How that works in practice is always very different. It's different if you're writing an email than writing a book, of course, but I think there are some common denominators of what really works here. I've always thought that you can explain the majority of good writing with the concept of brevity, that the person who says the most in the fewest words wins. And so much of what you consider bad writing is a good idea stuffed around endless, endless words and complexity that doesn't need to be there.

35
00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:27.060
And so I heard this idea many years ago that when you're writing, you should assume that every word costs you $100. And when you're writing, if you're just writing an email to someone, you should stop and say, does this word need to be here? Not, does this sentence make sense? Not, is this word grammatically correct? But just, is it absolutely necessary? If this word costs me $100, would I leave it or not? And so many times the answer is no. And you strip your communication down to what is really essential.

36
00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:56.580
That's a big part of it. Mark Twain, I think, was the one who said, when writing, leave out the parts that readers tend to skip. And you know when everything that you read, that you skip certain parts of it, leave those parts out. One thing that I found very helpful for me as a writer is the idea of writing for myself. I think most people are very good writers. If they were writing like in a diary where they didn't think anyone else was going to read it, it was for themselves. Or if they're note-taking, they're very good.

37
00:16:56.580 --> 00:17:23.860
at just jotting down the important information without a lot of fluff. They become bad writers when they start realizing that there might be an audience out there that other people are reading this. And then they either get nervous or they start to pander to other people. And so when I write, I'm glad the audience is there, the readers are there. I'm glad you're here right now. But you're never the most important thing. Whether it's this podcast or things that I write in books and whatnot,

38
00:17:23.860 --> 00:17:49.710
The only audience that I think about is myself. It's a very selfish way to write, but I think that has been what's most important. And when you have that frame of mind, you are willing to say things and take risks and have little rhetorical flourishes that you might be too scared to do otherwise. And so I think that's always been what's most important to me as a writer is that when I write anything, I want to take a step back and say, do I like this?

39
00:17:49.710 --> 00:18:18.400
Not will the audience appreciate this or enjoy this, but do I like it? And so that's been the strongest filter for me over time as a writer. Last, I just want to recommend something for you on this. And this is not a book or a podcast or a documentary as I've recommended before. Now that we're in the middle of the summer, with all seriousness, the most helpful thing that I've had in my career, and it's not even close, I think, is walking outside.

